Relevant tables / columns and how they will be used  

• actor.actor_id : will identify each actor.  
• actor.first_name / actor.last_name : will let us display actors’ names.  
• film_actor.film_id / film_actor.actor_id : connects actors to the films in which they appear.  
• inventory.film_id / inventory.inventory_id : maps each film to every physical copy that can be rented.  
• rental.inventory_id / rental.customer_id : tells us which customer actually rented a given copy (and therefore the film).  
• customer.customer_id : gives us the universe of all customers so we can compute percentages.

Typical join chain  
actor → film_actor → film → inventory → rental → customer  

Below are ten exploratory SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows where sensible) that progress from simple inspection to the final percentage calculation.

```sql
-- 1) List a sample of actors
SELECT DISTINCT actor_id, first_name, last_name
FROM actor
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) How many films has each actor appeared in?
SELECT fa.actor_id,
       COUNT(*) AS film_cnt
FROM film_actor AS fa
GROUP BY fa.actor_id
ORDER BY film_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Retrieve the current TOP‑5 actors by number of films
WITH films_per_actor AS (
  SELECT fa.actor_id,
         COUNT(*) AS film_cnt
  FROM film_actor AS fa
  GROUP BY fa.actor_id
)
SELECT a.actor_id,
       a.first_name,
       a.last_name,
       fpa.film_cnt
FROM films_per_actor AS fpa
JOIN actor AS a USING (actor_id)
ORDER BY fpa.film_cnt DESC
LIMIT 5;
```

```sql
-- 4) What films are attached to those TOP‑5 actors?
WITH top5 AS (
  SELECT actor_id
  FROM film_actor
  GROUP BY actor_id
  ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
  LIMIT 5
)
SELECT DISTINCT fa.actor_id, fa.film_id
FROM film_actor AS fa
JOIN top5  USING (actor_id)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Obtain every inventory copy of films featuring the TOP‑5 actors
WITH top5 AS (
  SELECT actor_id
  FROM film_actor
  GROUP BY actor_id
  ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
  LIMIT 5
),
top5_films AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT film_id
  FROM film_actor
  WHERE actor_id IN (SELECT actor_id FROM top5)
)
SELECT DISTINCT i.inventory_id, i.film_id
FROM inventory AS i
JOIN top5_films USING (film_id)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) All rentals that involve at least one of those inventory items
WITH top5 AS (
  SELECT actor_id
  FROM film_actor
  GROUP BY actor_id
  ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
  LIMIT 5
),
top5_films AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT film_id
  FROM film_actor
  WHERE actor_id IN (SELECT actor_id FROM top5)
),
top5_inventory AS (
  SELECT inventory_id
  FROM inventory
  WHERE film_id IN (SELECT film_id FROM top5_films)
)
SELECT DISTINCT r.rental_id,
       r.customer_id,
       r.inventory_id
FROM rental AS r
JOIN top5_inventory USING (inventory_id)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Count DISTINCT customers who ever rented a film with a TOP‑5 actor
WITH top5 AS (
  SELECT actor_id
  FROM film_actor
  GROUP BY actor_id
  ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
  LIMIT 5
),
top5_films AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT film_id
  FROM film_actor
  WHERE actor_id IN (SELECT actor_id FROM top5)
),
top5_inventory AS (
  SELECT inventory_id
  FROM inventory
  WHERE film_id IN (SELECT film_id FROM top5_films)
)
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT r.customer_id) AS customers_of_top5
FROM rental AS r
JOIN top5_inventory USING (inventory_id);
```

```sql
-- 8) Total number of customers in the database
SELECT COUNT(*) AS total_customers
FROM customer;
```

```sql
-- 9) Compute the percentage of customers who rented TOP‑5‑actor films
WITH
top5 AS (
  SELECT actor_id
  FROM film_actor
  GROUP BY actor_id
  ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
  LIMIT 5
),
top5_films AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT film_id
  FROM film_actor
  WHERE actor_id IN (SELECT actor_id FROM top5)
),
top5_inventory AS (
  SELECT inventory_id
  FROM inventory
  WHERE film_id IN (SELECT film_id FROM top5_films)
),
cust_top5 AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT r.customer_id
  FROM rental AS r
  JOIN top5_inventory USING (inventory_id)
),
totals AS (
  SELECT (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM cust_top5) AS top5_cust,
         (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customer) AS all_cust
)
SELECT top5_cust,
       all_cust,
       ROUND(100.0 * top5_cust / all_cust, 2) AS pct_of_customers
FROM totals;
```

```sql
-- 10) Same as #9 but presenting actors’ names alongside their reach
WITH
films_per_actor AS (
  SELECT fa.actor_id,
         COUNT(*) AS film_cnt
  FROM film_actor AS fa
  GROUP BY fa.actor_id
),
top5 AS (
  SELECT actor_id
  FROM films_per_actor
  ORDER BY film_cnt DESC
  LIMIT 5
),
customers_per_actor AS (
  SELECT fa.actor_id,
         COUNT(DISTINCT r.customer_id) AS cust_cnt
  FROM film_actor AS fa
  JOIN inventory i  USING (film_id)
  JOIN rental   r  USING (inventory_id)
  WHERE fa.actor_id IN (SELECT actor_id FROM top5)
  GROUP BY fa.actor_id
),
totals AS (
  SELECT COUNT(*) AS total_customers
  FROM customer
)
SELECT a.actor_id,
       a.first_name || ' ' || a.last_name AS actor_name,
       cpa.cust_cnt,
       t.total_customers,
       ROUND(100.0 * cpa.cust_cnt / t.total_customers, 2) AS pct_of_customers
FROM customers_per_actor AS cpa
JOIN actor AS a USING (actor_id)
CROSS JOIN totals AS t
ORDER BY pct_of_customers DESC;
```

The final queries (#9 and #10) show the overall percentage of customers who have rented at least one film featuring any of the top‑five actors, and also break the reach down by individual actor.
